## Session lifecycle (exact order)

1. Read constitutional authorities + `docs/project-health.json`
2. Read Engineering Milestone Report
3. Determine backlog state
4. Identify the next approved execution program
5. Verify prerequisites (including owner-review gates in project-health)
6. Implement **only** that program
7. Run repository verification
8. Run repository validation
9. Update `docs/project-health.json` (`pnpm sync:project-health`)
10. Update `docs/engineering-milestone-report.md` when the program completes engineering work
11. **STOP** — never auto-continue into another program

When Product Proof launch / human review is required by the program or by `project-health.json` handoff status:

Execution Program → Verification → Validation → **Launch** → **Human Review** → Milestone / health update → Next Execution Program

Do not skip human review when handoff status awaits owner Product Proof review.

## Artifact obligation (v1.1)

Every execution program MUST leave behind at least one of:

- a **new verifier** (`scripts/verify-*.mjs` wired into `pnpm test` or documented exception)
- a **new generator** (`scripts/sync-*.mjs` or Cargo export binary)
- a **new automation** (CI script, health sync, or equivalent deterministic tooling)

If none of these are produced, the Engineering Milestone Report MUST explain why (e.g. pure documentation convergence with no new machine check possible) and still update `docs/project-health.json`.

Prefer strengthening the repository’s self-validation over speculative features.

## Shall / Shall not

SHALL: Implement, verify, validate, document Product Proof, update project-health, stop.
SHALL NOT: Invent architecture/product/governance; expand scope; skip review/verification/validation; ship a provider without Conversation Product Proof; run multiple programs at once; skip owner Product Proof review when required.

If architectural uncertainty appears: STOP, document, recommend ADR + Constitutional Review, wait for owner approval.

